{:nav-title "Aggregations"
 :content
 [:div
  [:h1 "Aggregations"]
  [:p "Aggregation functions sit inside " [:code ":find"] " and collapse multiple rows into a single value. "
   "Any non-aggregated variable in " [:code ":find"] " becomes a grouping key — "
   "like SQL's " [:code "GROUP BY"] "."]

  [:h2 "count"]
  [:p "How many Pokemon belong to each type? "
   [:code "?type"] " is the grouping key; " [:code "(count ?name)"] " counts entries per group:"]
  [:ui/runnable "pokemon"
   [:find ?type (count ?name)
    :where
    [?e :pokemon/name ?name]
    [?e :pokemon/type ?type]]]

  [:h2 "min and max"]
  [:p "What is the highest and lowest speed in the Pokedex?"]
  [:ui/runnable "pokemon"
   [:find (min ?speed) (max ?speed)
    :where
    [?e :stat/speed ?speed]]]
  [:p "Add " [:code "?name"] " as a grouping key to see each Pokemon's speed alongside the extremes "
   "within its group — or move on to the Find Specifications chapter where you'll learn "
   "how to return a single value as a scalar."]

  [:h2 "avg and sum"]
  [:p [:code "avg"] " and " [:code "sum"] " look simple, but " [:strong "both queries below give the wrong answer"] ". "
   "The next section explains why. Run them anyway, and see whether you can spot the problem first."]
  [:p "Average attack across all Pokemon. Note the number you get:"]
  [:ui/runnable "pokemon"
   [:find (avg ?attack)
    :where
    [?e :stat/attack ?attack]]]
  [:p "Now add up the HP of every Pokemon — a rough measure of total bulk across the Pokedex. "
   "Before you run it, think about how many Pokemon there are and how much HP a typical one has, "
   "then guess the total:"]
  [:ui/runnable "pokemon"
   [:find (sum ?hp)
    :where
    [?e :stat/hp ?hp]]]
  [:p "The total is "
   [:ui/value "pokemon" [:find (sum ?hp) . :where [?e :stat/hp ?hp]]]
   " for "
   [:ui/value "pokemon" [:find (count ?e) . :where [?e :pokemon/name _]]]
   " Pokemon. That works out to about 20 HP each, far less than a typical Pokemon has. Something is off."]

  [:h2 "The :with clause"]
  [:p "The sum is too small because of how Datomic aggregates. "
   "Before aggregation, Datomic builds a " [:strong "set"] " from the " [:code ":find"] " variables. "
   "Rows that look identical after projecting onto those variables are merged. "
   "The HP query has only one variable, " [:code "?hp"] ", so two Pokemon with the same HP "
   "become a single row, and the second one is never added."]
  [:p "The " [:code ":with"] " clause fixes this. A variable in " [:code ":with"] " keeps rows apart "
   "before aggregation, but is not returned. Adding " [:code ":with ?e"] " keeps one row per Pokemon:"]
  [:ui/runnable "pokemon"
   [:find (sum ?hp)
    :with ?e
    :where
    [?e :stat/hp ?hp]]]
  [:p "The sum is now "
   [:ui/value "pokemon" [:find (sum ?hp) . :with ?e :where [?e :stat/hp ?hp]]]
   ". The average attack has the same problem, so here it is again with the fix:"]
  [:ui/runnable "pokemon"
   [:find (avg ?attack)
    :with ?e
    :where
    [?e :stat/attack ?attack]]]
  [:p "Compare it with the number you noted earlier: the average changed too. "
   "A sum that is far too small is easy to notice, but an average that is a couple of points off "
   "looks perfectly plausible. That is what makes this trap dangerous."]
  [:p "The same thing happens with " [:code "count"] ". Compare these two queries. "
   "The first counts " [:em "distinct type strings"] " in the database:"]
  [:ui/runnable "pokemon"
   [:find (count ?type)
    :where
    [?e :pokemon/type ?type]]]
  [:p "The second uses " [:code ":with"] " to include " [:code "?e"] " in the pre-aggregation set, "
   "preventing type strings from collapsing across entities. "
   "It counts total " [:em "type assignments"] " across all Pokemon:"]
  [:ui/runnable "pokemon"
   [:find (count ?type)
    :with ?e
    :where
    [?e :pokemon/type ?type]]]
  [:p "The first result, "
   [:ui/value "pokemon" [:find (count-distinct ?type) . :where [_ :pokemon/type ?type]]]
   ", is the number of unique type names. "
   "The second, "
   [:ui/value "pokemon" [:find (count ?type) . :with ?e :where [?e :pokemon/type ?type]]]
   ", is the total number of type slots filled across all "
   [:ui/value "pokemon" [:find (count ?e) . :where [?e :pokemon/name _]]]
   " Pokemon. It is higher because dual-typed Pokemon contribute two entries each."]
  [:p [:strong "When can you skip " [:code ":with"] "?"] " When rows cannot collapse, or when collapsing does not matter. "
   [:code "(count ?name)"] " in the " [:code "count"] " section was safe because Pokemon names are unique, "
   "so no two rows merge. " [:code "min"] " and " [:code "max"] " are immune because a duplicate never changes an extreme. "
   "For " [:code "sum"] ", " [:code "avg"] " and " [:code "count"] " over a value that different Pokemon can share, "
   "like HP, attack or type, add " [:code ":with ?e"] "."]

  [:h2 "count-distinct"]
  [:p [:code "count-distinct"] " counts unique values of a variable within each group, "
   "ignoring duplicates. Its difference from " [:code "count"] " becomes visible when "
   [:code ":with"] " introduces duplicate rows. "
   "These two queries group by type and measure speed values in each group. "
   "With " [:code ":with ?e"] ", the same speed can appear multiple times (once per Pokemon with that type). "
   [:code "count"] " tallies every speed slot; " [:code "count-distinct"] " collapses repeated speeds:"]
  [:ui/runnable "pokemon"
   [:find ?type (count ?speed)
    :with ?e
    :where
    [?e :pokemon/type ?type]
    [?e :stat/speed ?speed]]]
  [:p "Now the same query using " [:code "count-distinct"] " — for types where multiple Pokemon "
   "share a speed value, the count will be lower:"]
  [:ui/runnable "pokemon"
   [:find ?type (count-distinct ?speed)
    :with ?e
    :where
    [?e :pokemon/type ?type]
    [?e :stat/speed ?speed]]]
  [:ui/try-tip
   [:p "Add " [:code "?type"] " as a grouping key to the " [:code "avg"] " query with "
    [:code ":with ?e"] " to see average attack broken down by type. You will need a "
    [:code "[?e :pokemon/type ?type]"] " clause too, and if you drop " [:code ":with ?e"]
    " the Pokemon that share an attack within a type merge again. "
    "Or use " [:code "(min ?attack)"] " and " [:code "(max ?attack)"] " together to see the attack range per type. "
    "Those need no " [:code ":with"] "."]]]}
